<p>
This probably tracks my availability more than anything else. The dip in 2017 and 2018 corresponds pretty well to the time that the kiddo needed more of my attention. Babies sleep a lot; toddlers, not so much.
</p>

<p>
I generally feel that there are so many interesting rabbit holes to go down each week,
and the Emacs community is active enough that there's always one or two things I want to add to my to-do list to explore further.
Putting together Emacs News is one of the highlights of my week. <a href="https://sachachua.com/blog/2023/12/analyzing-my-emacs-time-over-the-last-11-years-or-so/">It takes me about 1 - 2 hours</a>,
and some of that is just because I get distracted trying out the cool stuff I come across.
</p>

<p>
Learning from similar newsletters: <a href="https://alan.petitepomme.net/cwn/">OCaml Weekly News</a> has a single author as well and does a lot more summarization, but includes fewer links.
<a href="https://this-week-in-rust.org/">This Week in Rust</a> is a similar link-dump weekly newsletter.
It's written by a larger team and it has more commentary.
I think that for the Emacs community, I'm still leaning towards this large list of categorized links for easy skimming.
As I get the hang of writing more, I'd like to write more blog posts myself that Emacs News can also link to.
If your community doesn't already have a weekly or monthly newsletter, I highly recommend it as a way to <a href="https://sachachua.com/blog/2025/05/working-on-the-plumbing-in-a-small-web-community/">bring the community together</a>.
</p>

